! ===========================================================================
! B7.6 example 3 -- A | vacuum, fcc Cu(111), with the GENERATED vacuum lead
! ===========================================================================
!
! A surface, built on the layered (calctype='L') path: one fcc Cu(111) active
! layer with a frozen Cu reference below it and SEMI-INFINITE VACUUM above.
!
! WHAT MAKES THIS DIFFERENT FROM ../fccCu111_AA
!
! In the A | A and A | B geometries both frozen regions are metals, and both
! parameter sets are read from &atoms label(:) files produced by earlier
! converged bulk runs. Here region B is vacuum, and its frozen parameters are
! not read from anywhere -- they are GENERATED, per run, by
! source/vacuum_lead.f90:
!
!   &lattice region_b_kind = 'vacuum'
!
! An empty sphere of the run's own Wigner-Seitz radius, holding a constant
! potential, is set up as an ordinary Z = 0 atom and pushed through the code's
! own radial solver (self%potpar). The representation, the gamma/o convention,
! the E_nu convention and the normalization therefore come out automatically
! consistent with every other parameter set in the run -- which is the whole
! reason B7 §1.6 insists on the solver rather than hand-coded spherical-Bessel
! expressions. The analytic Bessel result is the test ORACLE
! (tests/unit/test_vacuum_lead.f90), never the implementation.
!
! THE VACUUM LEVEL IS SELF-CONSISTENT, NOT A KNOB
!
! The constant potential V0 inside those empty spheres is the vacuum level,
! and it is not an input. It is the alignment shift the B7.4 solver already
! maintains for every non-anchor region: the level at which deep vacuum sees
! zero deviation field. Each SCF iteration, `interfacepot` solves for it and
! then regenerates the vacuum parameters at the new value (the
! `on_alignment_updated` hook). Iteration 0 runs at V0 = 0, which is exactly
! the old "generate once from a given vacuum level" behaviour -- it arrives
! here as the first step of the self-consistent scheme rather than as a
! separate code path.
!
! Removing that hand-set knob is one of the two things B7 §1.6 says the vacuum
! lead is actually FOR. The other is a well-defined vacuum level. Note what is
! deliberately NOT claimed: see the next section.
!
! WHAT THIS EXAMPLE WILL *NOT* SHOW, AND WHY THAT IS THE POINT
!
! It will not move the converged surface numbers much. For a good metal the
! evanescent decay constant is kappa = sqrt(Phi) ~ 0.54 bohr^-1 at Phi ~ 4 eV,
! so vacuum tails die within ~2 AA. THAT is why one or two hand-set empty-
! sphere layers already worked. B7 §1.6 is explicit that the acceptance
! criterion for the vacuum lead is REDUCED SENSITIVITY TO THE VACUUM LAYER
! COUNT, not a changed answer.
!
! So if you diff this against example/surface/fccCu111/Single and find close
! agreement, that is the expected result, not a null result. A large
! disagreement would be the thing worth investigating.
!
! The regime where the vacuum lead genuinely earns its keep is elsewhere:
! vacuum-gap interlayer exchange, tunnelling barriers, and low-DOS spacers,
! where the tail is the physics rather than a boundary detail.
!
! ONE LIMITATION, STATED HONESTLY (B7 §1.6)
!
! LMTO-ASA reproduces empty-lattice free-electron dispersion only over a
! limited window above the band bottom. The frozen vacuum parameters are
! meaningful only while E_F sits well below the vacuum band onset. That holds
! comfortably for ordinary metals such as Cu; it is uncomfortable for very low
! work functions. The run checks the margin every time it regenerates and warns
! when it is thin -- if you see that warning, treat vacuum-side results with
! corresponding caution rather than ignoring it.
!
! BUFFER WIDTH -- the two nlay_a/nlay_b pairs
!
! Same trap as in ../fccCu111_AA, and worth re-reading there in full:
!   &lattice nlay_a/nlay_b  count ATOMIC LAYERS -- the frozen-buffer knob.
!   &charge  nlay_a/nlay_b  count ROWS of the synthetic 2D Madelung stack.
! Raise the &lattice pair to thicken the buffer; leave the &charge pair UNSET
! so the active zone is centred in the Madelung stack automatically.
!
! RELATION TO THE OTHER EXAMPLES
!
!   ../fccCu111_AA              A | A identity (both boundaries metallic)
!   ../fccCu111_AB_misaligned   alignment solver vs an injected offset
!   this one                    A | vacuum, generated vacuum lead
!   ../../surface/fccCu111/Single   the same surface via 'buildsurf', which
!                               B7 leaves untouched as the permanent
!                               regression oracle for this geometry.
! ===========================================================================
&calculation
pre_processing = 'buildinterface'
verbose = T
/
&lattice
rc = 30
alat = 3.614
crystal_sym = 'fcc'
pbc = .true.
n1 = 16
n2 = 16
n3 = 16
wav = 1.41237
nbulk_bulk = 1            ! inequivalent sites per cell in region A
nlay = 1                  ! active layers
nlay_a = 1                ! frozen region A width, in ATOMIC LAYERS
nlay_b = 1                ! vacuum width, in ATOMIC LAYERS
region_b_kind = 'vacuum'  ! <-- region B is vacuum; its parameters are
                          !     generated per run, not read from a label.
surftype = '1 1 1'
ntype = 3                 ! A + vacuum + one type per active layer
ct(:) = 3*4.0d0
r2 = 16.0d0
/
&atoms
database = './'
! Label order is load bearing: frozen blocks first in region order, then one
! self-consistent type per active layer.
!
! Vac.nml is a SLOT, not a data source. Its potential parameters are
! overwritten by the generator before the first recursion and again after
! every alignment update; only its element block (Z = 0, no valence) is
! meaningful. It exists because the &atoms reader requires a file per label.
label(1) = 'A'            ! frozen region A reference (bulk Cu)
label(2) = 'Vac'          ! vacuum slot -- parameters generated, see above
label(3) = 'Ac1'          ! active layer 1
/
&self
nstep = 30
/
&energy
fermi = -0.089874
energy_min = -1.5
energy_max = 2.0
channels_ldos = 2000
fix_fermi = .true.
/
&control
calctype = 'L'            ! layered / interface
nsp = 1
lld = 150
recur = 'chebyshev'
/
&mix
beta = 0.0020
mixtype = 'broyden'
/
&charge
! ROW counts in the synthetic Madelung stack -- NOT atomic layers, and
! deliberately separate from the &lattice entries above.
!
! LEFT UNSET ON PURPOSE. The active zone must be CENTRED in the stack, or the
! two deep probes sit at very different distances from the active charge and
! the alignment solver reports a nonzero V_B for a physically symmetric cell.
! Unset, build_interface_registry centres it automatically -- (nbas - nlay)/2
! either side. Set these only for a deliberately asymmetric stack.
/
